.TH WPAKEY 2
.SH NAME
wpakey \- WPA2-PSK key derivation and the EAPOL four-way handshake
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "wpakey.m";
wpakey := load Wpakey Wpakey->PATH;

Asend, Actl, Adelay: con iota;

Action: adt {
    kind:  int;
    frame: array of byte;
    text:  string;
    ms:    int;
};

Supp: adt {
    pmk, smac, amac, rsne, ptk: array of byte;
    lastrepc: big;
    newptk:   int;

    mk:    fn(pmk, smac, rsne: array of byte): ref Supp;
    reset: fn(s: self ref Supp);
    recv:  fn(s: self ref Supp, frame, snonce: array of byte):
               (list of ref Action, string);
};

init:        fn();
pbkdf2_sha1: fn(pass, salt: array of byte, rounds, dklen: int): array of byte;
psk:         fn(passphrase, essid: string): array of byte;
prf:         fn(key: array of byte, label: string, seed: array of byte,
                 nbits: int): array of byte;
ptk:         fn(pmk, smac, amac, snonce, anonce: array of byte): array of byte;
mic:         fn(vers: int, kck, frame: array of byte): array of byte;
aescmac:     fn(key, msg: array of byte): array of byte;
micname:     fn(vers: int): string;
aesunwrap:   fn(kek, data: array of byte): array of byte;
rsnie:       fn(): array of byte;
hex:         fn(a: array of byte): string;
unhex:       fn(s: string): array of byte;
eaddr:       fn(a: array of byte): string;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Wpakey
holds the arithmetic and the state machine of a WPA2 personal
supplicant.
It opens no file, reads no clock and installs no key:
.B recv
takes one received EAPOL frame and returns the list of things its
caller must then do.
.IR wpa (8)
is the program that does them.
The split is what lets a test drive an entire association with no radio.
.PP
.B Init
loads
.IR keyring (2)
and must be called first.
.PP
.B Pbkdf2_sha1
is PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA1 as its pseudorandom function (RFC 2898
section 5.2).
The password and salt are byte arrays rather than strings because the
published test vectors contain NUL bytes.
.B Psk
is the pairwise master key of a WPA2 personal network: 4096 rounds of
.B pbkdf2_sha1
over the passphrase, salted with the network name, to 32 bytes
(IEEE 802.11i-2004 Annex H.4).
.PP
.B Prf
is the IEEE 802.11 pseudorandom function: HMAC-SHA1 of the label, a NUL,
the seed and a counter byte, concatenated until
.I nbits
are available.
Every output is a prefix of every longer one.
.B Ptk
derives the 64-byte pairwise transient key from the master key, the two
ethernet addresses and the two nonces; the seed orders each pair so that
both ends compute the same thing without agreeing which is which.
The result is the key confirmation key (bytes 0-15), the key encryption
key (16-31) and the CCMP temporal key (32-47).
.PP
.B Mic
is the message integrity check over a whole EAPOL frame whose own MIC
field has been zeroed, keyed by the key confirmation key.
The key descriptor version chooses the algorithm: version 1 is
HMAC-MD5 (WPA1 with TKIP), version 2 is HMAC-SHA1 (WPA2 with CCMP) and
version 3 is AES-128-CMAC, which is what an access point asks for when
protected management frames are in play (IEEE 802.11-2016 12.7.2).
Any other version returns
.BR nil ,
which is how
.B recv
tells a version it does not implement from a frame it merely dislikes.
.B Micname
gives the same versions in words, so that a refusal can name what was
asked for.
.PP
.B Aescmac
is AES-CMAC (RFC 4493) on its own, exported so that the published
vectors can reach it directly.
It is CBC-MAC with the last block corrected by one of two subkeys
derived by doubling the encryption of a zero block in GF(2^128);
which subkey depends on whether the message fills its last block.
A key that is not a valid AES length returns
.BR nil .
.PP
.B Aesunwrap
is AES key unwrap (RFC 3394 section 2.2.2), which is how message 3 of
the handshake carries the group key.
It returns
.B nil
if the integrity check fails \(em a wrong key encryption key, or a
tampered wrap.
.PP
.B Rsnie
returns the RSN information element this supplicant offers: WPA2, CCMP
for both ciphers, PSK authentication.
.PP
.B Supp.mk
makes a supplicant for one master key, one station address and the RSN
element that was claimed when associating.
.B Reset
clears the replay counter, the derived key and the
key-installation gate; call it whenever the link drops, because none of
those is meaningful across an association.
.PP
.B Recv
takes a received ethernet frame, complete with its 14-byte header, and
.I snonce
\(em 32 fresh random bytes, which the caller supplies on every call and
which are used only if the frame turns out to be message 1.
Randomness is thus the caller's business, and this module has no opinion
about where it comes from.
It returns the actions to perform, in order, and a diagnostic.
An action is one of:
.TF Adelay
.TP
.B Asend
write
.B frame
to the conversation's data file.
.TP
.B Actl
write
.B text
to its ctl file.
.I Text
contains key material and must not be logged.
.TP
.B Adelay
wait
.B ms
milliseconds before the next action.
.PD
.PP
A frame that is simply not this station's business returns no actions
and no diagnostic.
A frame that was addressed to this station and looked like part of a
handshake but could not be used returns no actions and a diagnostic:
a bad integrity check, a stale replay counter, key data that did not
unwrap, a truncated frame, or a key descriptor version this module does
not implement.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/wpakey.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR keyring (2),
.IR wpa (8)
.PP
Plan 9's
.B aux/wpa
is the specification this was written from.
.SH BUGS
Only WPA2 personal with CCMP is implemented.
.B Recv
refuses key descriptor version 1 (WPA1/TKIP) with a diagnostic:
the RC4 unwrap of its key data is not implemented, so accepting the
earlier messages would only fail later and less clearly.
Versions 2 and 3 are both accepted \(em they differ only in the
integrity check and wrap their key data identically \(em but accepting
version 3 does not amount to supporting protected management frames,
which
.B rsnie
does not offer.
